1	# Chain of Responsibility
2	
3	Pass a request along a sequence of handlers; each one either handles it, or passes it on, or stops it.
4	
5	## Use it when
6	
7	- several handlers could deal with a request, and which one does depends on the request;
8	- the set of handlers or their order changes per configuration;
9	- new handlers are expected, and today each one would add a branch to a central method.
10	
11	## Do not use it when
12	
13	- there is always exactly one handler: call it;
14	- every handler must always run around the same operation and add something: that is closer to Decorator;
15	- the order and the set never change and fit in one short method.
16	
17	## Shape
18	
19	```java
20	interface ClickHandler {
21	    boolean handle(Click click);           // true when handled; false to let the next one try
22	}
23	
24	final class HandlerChain {
25	    private final List<ClickHandler> handlers;      // the order is decided where the chain is built
26	    HandlerChain(List<ClickHandler> handlers) { this.handlers = List.copyOf(handlers); }
27	
28	    void dispatch(Click click) {
29	        for (ClickHandler handler : handlers) {
30	            if (handler.handle(click)) return;
31	        }
32	    }
33	}
34	
35	final class ButtonHandler implements ClickHandler { /* handles clicks inside the button */ }
36	final class PanelHandler implements ClickHandler { /* handles what the button did not */ }
37	final class WindowHandler implements ClickHandler { /* the last resort */ }
38	```
39	
40	A linked form (each handler holds `next`) is the same pattern.
41	
42	## Done right when
43	
44	- each handler is its own piece and decides only about its own case;
45	- the list (or links) is built in one place, where the configuration is known;
46	- the dispatcher knows no handler by name;
47	- adding a handler means one new class and one change where the chain is built.
48	
49	## Usual mistakes
50	
51	- Handlers check a global setting to decide whether they are part of the chain, instead of the builder leaving them
52	  out.
53	- The dispatcher still has special cases for some requests before the chain.
54	- A handler depends on a flag set by a previous one in a hidden way.
55	
56	## Neighbors
57	
58	- **Decorator**: every wrapper runs and adds behavior; in a chain, usually one handler takes the request.
59	- **Strategy**: one rule chosen up front; a chain tries handlers in order.
60

1	# State
2	
3	Represent each state of an object as its own class, so the object behaves differently as its state changes, without
4	`switch` statements on the state in every method.
5	
6	## Use it when
7	
8	- one object moves through states over time, and several of its operations behave differently in each state;
9	- the transitions themselves carry rules (what is allowed from where, what happens on entering a state);
10	- adding a state today would mean editing many methods that switch on the current state.
11	
12	## Do not use it when
13	
14	- the "state" is just a status field that is read, with one or two simple checks: an enum and a guard clause are
15	  clearer;
16	- the allowed transitions fit in a small table (from, event, to) and the behavior does not differ per state;
17	- the variant is chosen once and never changes: that is Strategy.
18	
19	## Shape
20	
21	```java
22	interface MachineState {
23	    MachineState insertCoin(VendingMachine m, int cents);
24	    MachineState selectItem(VendingMachine m, String code);
25	}
26	
27	final class Idle implements MachineState { /* insertCoin -> HasCredit; selectItem -> refuse */ }
28	final class HasCredit implements MachineState { /* selectItem -> Dispensing or refuse */ }
29	final class Dispensing implements MachineState { /* everything refused until done */ }
30	
31	final class VendingMachine {
32	    private MachineState state = new Idle();
33	    void insertCoin(int cents) { state = state.insertCoin(this, cents); }
34	    void selectItem(String code) { state = state.selectItem(this, code); }
35	}
36	```
37	
38	## Done right when
39	
40	- each state class decides what its operations do and which state comes next;
41	- the context object only delegates to its current state;
42	- adding a state means one new class and the transitions that lead to it.
43	
44	## Usual mistakes
45	
46	- State classes exist, but the context still checks `if (state instanceof …)` before delegating.
47	- Most methods in most states only throw "not allowed": a transition table would have been simpler.
48	- Using State for something that never changes during the object's life.
49	
50	## Neighbors
51	
52	- **Strategy**: chosen from outside, stays fixed. State is changed by the object itself.
53	- **A transition table**: enough when states differ only in which transitions are allowed.
54

